+import { Callout } from 'nextra/components'
+
+# Semantic contracts and deterministic profile composition
+
+<Callout type="warning">
+**Draft companion proposal for Signet 2.** This document makes the "shared meaning" and translation-profile boundaries explicit enough for independent implementations to reproduce the same session contract. Names and fields remain open to revision.
+</Callout>
+
+## Why this exists
+
+Signet 2 proposes the right high-level direction:
+
+~~~text
+game
+-> shared meaning
+-> authoritative server
+-> shared meaning
+-> game
+~~~
+
+The remaining protocol problem is defining exactly what counts as the same "shared meaning".
+
+Human-readable labels are useful for documentation, but they are not enough for protocol identity.
+
+~~~text
+SHARED_LABELS != SHARED_SEMANTICS
+~~~
+
+Two implementations can both use the label `fire` while disagreeing about whether it means a request, an accepted action, a held state, a one-shot edge, an operation subject to cooldown, or an already authoritative effect.
+
+This proposal therefore treats shared meaning as a versioned semantic contract.
+
+## Semantic references
+
+Every normative semantic item has a stable reference.
+
+Illustrative shape:
+
+~~~json
+{
+  "namespace": "signet.core",
+  "id": "intent.fire",
+  "version": 1,
+  "definition_hash": "sha256:..."
+}
+~~~
+
+A semantic definition should publish:
+
+- a stable identity;
+- a version;
+- a canonical definition hash;
+- a prose definition;
+- examples;
+- non-examples;
+- authority assumptions;
+- ordering or visibility assumptions when relevant;
+- conformance assertions.
+
+A display label can change without changing protocol identity.
+
+A semantic definition cannot change incompatibly while retaining the same identity and version.
+
+## Core plus profiles
+
+The universal core should stay small.
+
+Game families can activate additional versioned semantic profiles:
+
+~~~text
+signet.core
+movement.realtime
+combat.ranged
+inventory.basic
+vehicle.basic
+turn-based.actions
+card-game.hidden-information
+...
+~~~
+
+A game does not need to implement unrelated profiles to participate.
+
+This avoids turning the first supported game family into the implicit ontology of every future game.
+
+## Archetype identity versus authoritative rules
+
+An archetype identifies semantic role or category.
+
+It does not by itself define every authoritative parameter.
+
+For example:
+
+~~~text
+archetype:
+  weapon.ranged
+
+authoritative ruleset instance:
+  damage = 20
+  range = 40
+  cooldown = 0.5
+~~~
+
+The server owns authoritative ruleset parameters and state.
+
+The local translator owns presentation of the archetype.
+
+## Capability offers
+
+A translator sends a capability offer.
+
+A capability offer describes what that participant can legitimately emit, observe or present.
+
+Illustrative shape:
+
+~~~json
+{
+  "profiles": [
+    {
+      "id": "https://signetprotocol.io/semantics/movement/realtime/1",
+      "definition_hash": "sha256:..."
+    }
+  ],
+  "can_emit": ["intent.move", "intent.turn", "intent.fire"],
+  "can_present": ["weapon.ranged", "health_pickup"],
+  "limitations": ["intent.jump"]
+}
+~~~
+
+A capability offer is not the negotiated session contract.
+
+## Session requirements
+
+The session or active ruleset declares what it needs.
+
+Illustrative shape:
+
+~~~json
+{
+  "required_profiles": [
+    {
+      "id": "https://signetprotocol.io/semantics/movement/realtime/1",
+      "definition_hash": "sha256:..."
+    }
+  ],
+  "required_capabilities": ["intent.move", "intent.turn"],
+  "optional_capabilities": ["intent.jump"],
+  "ordering_preferences": [
+    {
+      "id": "https://signetprotocol.io/semantics/ordering/fixed-tick/1",
+      "definition_hash": "sha256:..."
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "https://signetprotocol.io/semantics/ordering/event-sequence/1",
+      "definition_hash": "sha256:..."
+    }
+  ]
+}
+~~~
+
+## Negotiated session contract
+
+The server derives a deterministic contract from:
+
+~~~text
+participant capability offers
++
+session requirements
++
+active semantic profiles
++
+explicit fallback policy
+~~~
+
+The result records at least:
+
+- exact semantic/profile identities;
+- required capabilities;
+- optional capabilities;
+- activated capabilities;
+- rejected capabilities;
+- typed fallbacks;
+- selected ordering model;
+- selected visibility policy when relevant;
+- ruleset identity;
+- authority model;
+- deterministic admission result.
+
+Useful capability outcomes include:
+
+~~~text
+SUPPORTED
+SUPPORTED_WITH_FALLBACK
+OBSERVE_ONLY
+INCOMPATIBLE
+~~~
+
+## Semantic acceptability versus session preference
+
+A reusable semantic profile should say which models preserve its semantics.
+
+The session should say which acceptable model it prefers.
+
+~~~text
+profile:
+  what is semantically acceptable?
+
+session:
+  among acceptable choices, what do we prefer?
+
+negotiation:
+  select the first session preference accepted by every activated profile
+~~~
+
+Example:
+
+~~~text
+session preferences:
+  fixed-tick@1
+  event-sequence@1
+  turn-sequence@1
+
+profile A accepts:
+  fixed-tick@1
+  event-sequence@1
+
+profile B accepts:
+  event-sequence@1
+  turn-sequence@1
+
+selected:
+  event-sequence@1
+~~~
+
+If no preference is accepted by every activated profile, negotiation fails with a machine-readable conflict.
+
+Implementations must not derive semantic policy from local enumeration order.
+
+## Optional profiles
+
+Optional semantics also need deterministic policy.
+
+"Optional" does not mean "enable it if installed".
+
+Activating one optional profile can:
+
+- introduce a dependency;
+- collide with another concept;
+- narrow an ordering-model intersection;
+- narrow a visibility-policy intersection;
+- conflict with an earlier optional profile.
+
+A simple deterministic algorithm is:
+
+1. compose the required baseline;
+2. iterate optional profiles in declared session preference order;
+3. tentatively add one candidate;
+4. resolve its required dependencies;
+5. rerun complete semantic composition;
+6. rerun ordering and visibility selection;
+7. keep the candidate if the full contract remains valid;
+8. otherwise skip it and record an exact reason.
+
+Earlier accepted optional profiles remain preferred.
+
+Example result:
+
+~~~text
+activated:
+  optional-event@1
+
+skipped:
+  optional-fixed@1
+    ORDERING_MODEL_CONFLICT
+
+  optional-missing@1
+    REQUIRED_PROFILE_UNSUPPORTED
+~~~
+
+This algorithm is deliberately policy-driven rather than mathematically maximal.
+
+The session's declared preference order is the objective.
+
+## Typed fallback
+
+A fallback that changes only local presentation is not the same as a fallback that changes shared simulation semantics.
+
+A fallback record should identify:
+
+- source semantic;
+- target semantic or no-op;
+- fallback class;
+- information loss;
+- whether shared simulation changes;
+- authority that approved it;
+- conformance evidence.
+
+Illustrative classes:
+
+~~~text
+PRESENTATION_SUBSTITUTION
+INPUT_REBIND
+LOSSY_OBSERVATION
+SIMULATION_SUBSTITUTION
+NO_OP
+~~~
+
+A simulation-affecting fallback must be explicitly allowed by the active semantic profile or session ruleset.
+
+Unknown is preferable to invented precision.
+
+## Translation-profile authority classes
+
+A translation profile can contain several classes of decision.
+
+They do not have one universal owner.
+
+| Class | Primary authority | Shared-state impact |
+|---|---|---:|
+| input binding | participant / adapter | indirect |
+| motion observation | measured adapter evidence | indirect |
+| semantic mapping | translator/profile authority | yes |
+| appearance mapping | participant/client | no |
+| session policy | server/session | yes |
+
+A player's appearance preference is not equivalent to unilateral authority to redefine an authoritative semantic mapping.
+
+## Publisher baseline and participant overlay
+
+Separate publisher-owned and participant-owned state:
+
+~~~text
+publisher_profile
+  immutable/versioned baseline distributed with translator
+
+participant_overlay
+  local participant-owned bindings, pins and explicit preferences
+
+session_resolution
+  derived effective profile for one exact dependency cut
+~~~
+
+A publisher update may change publisher defaults.
+
+It must not silently rewrite participant-owned decisions.
+
+If an old participant entry becomes incompatible with a new semantic definition, the correct result is explicit reconfirmation or invalidation.
+
+## Pinned versus current
+
+~~~text
+PINNED_CHOICE != CORRECT_CHOICE
+~~~
+
+Pinning establishes repeatability inside one profile revision.
+
+It does not prove that dependencies have not changed.
+
+A simulation-relevant mapping should bind to:
+
+- game/version identity;
+- integration surface identity or digest;
+- semantic definition;
+- transform identity;
+- candidate-set fingerprint when relevant;
+- resolver identity when relevant;
+- conformance or replay evidence.
+
+Suggested freshness states:
+
+~~~text
+VALID
+SUSPECT
+REVALIDATING
+INVALID
+~~~
+
+## Effective profile identity
+
+Installed package bytes are not the whole behavior-bearing state.
+
+~~~text
+PACKAGE_BYTES != EFFECTIVE_PROFILE
+~~~
+
+The effective profile can depend on:
+
+- publisher baseline;
+- participant overlay;
+- active semantic profiles;
+- session policy;
+- calibration results;
+- fallback approvals.
+
+A reproducible session should therefore have an `effective_profile_digest` over the resolved behavior-bearing profile cut.
+
+That digest can be referenced by:
+
+- replay metadata;
+- compatibility evidence;
+- incident reports;
+- support diagnostics.
+
+## Canonical composition
+
+If multiple artifacts compose into the effective contract, composition becomes protocol-relevant.
+
+The protocol should define:
+
+- canonical semantic reference ordering;
+- dependency resolution;
+- duplicate handling;
+- conflict handling;
+- optional activation order;
+- canonical serialization;
+- canonical hashing.
+
+Where array order is not semantic, canonicalization must not let input order change the digest.
+
+Where order is semantic, such as a declared preference list, canonicalization must preserve it.
+
+## Conformance vectors
+
+The semantic contract should ship executable test vectors for:
+
+- canonical hashing;
+- required profile composition;
+- deterministic ordering selection;
+- deterministic visibility selection;
+- deterministic optional activation;
+- exact failure reasons;
+- contract serialization;
+- negative conflict cases.
+
+Independent implementations should reproduce the same result hashes.
+
+A useful property is that vectors can also falsify the specification artifact itself.
+
+If an expected fixture is wrong, a correct reference implementation should expose that mismatch.
+
+## Resolver boundary
+
+Resolvers may assist development or calibration.
+
+They do not own protocol meaning.
+
+~~~text
+MODEL_SUGGESTION != AUTHORITY
+~~~
+
+Resolver output should be one of:
+
+~~~text
+ranked candidates
+NO_MATCH
+~~~
+
+A closed candidate set prevents out-of-list invention.
+
+It does not prove that the correct answer is present.
+
+Simulation-affecting unresolved mappings should fail closed or require explicit authority to pin them.
+
+## Minimal interoperability claim
+
+Two independent implementations should be able to start with the same:
+
+- semantic definitions;
+- participant capability offers;
+- session requirements;
+- profile dependencies;
+
+and derive the same:
+
+- activated semantic profiles;
+- selected ordering/visibility policy;
+- optional-profile decisions;
+- typed fallbacks;
+- effective contract hash.
+
+If they do not, the specification is missing a rule.
+
+## Open decisions
+
+This proposal intentionally leaves several choices open for Signet governance:
+
+- exact semantic-reference URI syntax;
+- exact canonical serialization format;
+- whether definition hashes use RFC 8785 JSON canonicalization or another fixed representation;
+- whether session contracts are signed;
+- which conflict reasons are mandatory in Signet 2;
+- which profiles belong in the initial core distribution;
+- migration rules for compatible semantic successors.
+
+Those are smaller questions once the authority and determinism boundaries are explicit.
+
